Show native views on Wayland: the app's wl_surface as a subsurface below the window, its hole cut by the renderer.

What: `NativeView::new` takes the `wl_proxy` of a role-less `wl_surface` the app made on GPUI's own connection (the
window's display handle), as macOS takes an `NSView`. GPUI gives it a `wp_viewport` at once and, the first frame that
paints the view, a `wl_subsurface` of the window's surface: `place_below(parent)`, synchronized, an empty input region
(all input stays on GPUI's surface: patch 0048's `NativeViewInput::Window`). Each frame, before the renderer commits
the window, a view whose placement changed is synchronized, `set_position` and `wp_viewport.set_destination` take the
element's bounds rounded outwards to whole logical pixels, and its surface is committed (cached until the window's
commit), so the move, resize, show or hide lands in the frame that moves the hole; once the window has committed, the
view goes back to desynchronized, so the app's own commits show at once and never wake GPUI's frame loop. A view no
longer painted gets a null buffer, in step with the window's commit, and keeps its role (Chromium's mutter workaround:
mutter may never release a buffer whose surface lost its role); painted again, it gets a fresh subsurface, as
Chromium's `Show` does. At a fractional scale the hole is cut one physical pixel inside the view (patch 0047's
`set_native_view_hole_inset`), so the window covers the rounded edges. An opaque window's opaque region leaves out the
shown views. Dropping the last `NativeView` handle destroys the subsurface and viewport. `wl_subcompositor` is bound
when offered; without it or `wp_viewporter`, `NativeView::new` fails as before.

New file `wayland/window/native_view.rs`, ported from chromium/chromium ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/
wayland_subsurface.cc and wayland_frame_manager.cc @ 4ea2b624ed0c500ec2f23ff17203dff91ad8faa2 (BSD-3-Clause,
Copyright 2020 The Chromium Authors; LICENSE-CHROMIUM, NOTICE, third_party/chromium/PORTED.md, which lists what was
dropped). Table tests: `steps` (placement × frame → step) and `subsurface_bounds_round_outwards`. `window.rs` holds
only the hooks; `client.rs` binds `wl_subcompositor`. A port, so one patch whatever its size (patch rule 4).

Why: `NativeView::new` failed on every platform but macOS, so a browser shell (wubble) had no zero-overhead page
view on Linux (xuckless/parallax#1). The window's surface and frame commit are private to gpui_linux.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

